            <ArticleTitle>Dyslipidaemia and coronary heart disease: nature vs nurture.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In order to enhance health care for patients with coronary heart disease (CHD), genetic markers of susceptibility could be incorporated into a formula for risk evaluation that includes traditional factors. Preventive measures could then be targeted towards 'high-risk' subjects. But can the genetic component be dissected from the environmental component in an intermediate CHD phenotype, such as plasma lipoproteins.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
